    {
      "question": "e-419",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "aada2aada75b4547d22747fdb43dc9d2cc813b2224d319e6777eac6cefcc4343",
      "new_sha256": "44cb9f1457a3f72af4db59ae26239bb23cdabd2320be3f30972171d134db88ab",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-419",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{6}^{0}(\\frac{1}{3}f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{6}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{6}^{0}1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 54+x\\,{|}_{6}^{0} \\\\  & =18+(0-6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/example-419.json",
          "file_sha256": "fe168f882e5a059aa3206f5829677a51982f686e27d37d3d40c67f24d763e637"
        }
