    {
      "question": "e-420",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "0135fefbcb8e5faa83f0833e81f6e004d76318a3a0c074a0a0f12f644e8966d4",
      "new_sha256": "391fd90b8c5bcb04f8f3c67ac46d9ba8e0ba120c1f8c643a0e5e17c2fc2c5e03",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-420",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{7}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_7^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{10}f(x)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information into the above gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+18={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 12={\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute the desired integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{10}(2f(x)+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{10}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{10}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(12)+x\\,{|}_{0}^{10} \\\\  & =24+(10-0) \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/example-420.json",
          "file_sha256": "aff1d27d896ae722ca35f75eac66fb135da04324c16c564bda461b495f9b7e97"
