    {
      "question": "e-421",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "f19248759a6defc030052f96b42f37d22e923aa5559e656e85abfadb258a99ae",
      "new_sha256": "5a8fbdfbb3607064a6b8a783ee1768d5d3dedd6ba5ae59d37336a6880a006a66",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-421",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let's simplify the first given integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{1}2\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ 2{\\int }_{4}^{1}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ -2{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ -{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2 \\\\ {\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, from the adjacent intervals rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/example-421.json",
          "file_sha256": "cf98b0a8b5b4b5181da2ccb5ba6bec4aa73b1a2327794f9664a5e0603683945f"
